diff --git a/fs/encfs/README.md b/fs/encfs/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..76ccd72317c911009a3e66fdc3781b3e26cd81a0
--- /dev/null
+++ b/fs/encfs/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,167 @@
+# EncFS master rotation and external key providers
+
+`CONFIG_ENCFS_KEYWRAP` adds authenticated external keyslots. It is optional:
+the existing board unique-key and directly derived legacy-volume path keeps
+the same on-disk format and derivation. Legacy volumes explicitly return
+`-ENOTSUP` from `encfs_rekey()`. Wrapping a key derived from an old master
+would not revoke that master's ability to derive the data key, so this
+implementation does not pretend that operation is a secure migration.
+
+For a new wrapped volume, explicit `encfs_provision()` obtains 64 fresh,
+independent random bytes from the selected provider. The first 32 bytes are
+the stable block key; the second 32 bytes are the stable path key. They are
+never derived from a master version. The provider wraps those data keys in
+a separate authenticated keyslot. `encfs_rekey(new_master_id)` can replace
+that wrapper while the volume is READY and files/directories remain open.
+It never changes an active XTS key, filename key, filesystem mount, or user
+data. The same provider key identifier returns `-EALREADY`.
+
+## Provider contract
+
+Install `struct encfs_key_provider_s` using `encfs_set_key_provider()` while
+fully STOPPED. A board can implement the callbacks with a trusted keystore
+or service. The callbacks execute under the manager lock and must not
+re-enter it. Their context must outlive registration.
+
+* `get_master(id)` supplies exactly 32 bytes for an immutable master version.
+  Different identifiers must denote distinct strong master keys. A missing
+  key returns `-ENOKEY`; returning a positive value is treated as `-EIO`.
+* `random()` supplies fresh cryptographic randomness for each new wrapped
+  volume. Failure aborts provision instead of using an unseeded fallback.
+* `read_slot()` returns exactly the 112-byte record, or `-ENOENT`.
+* `compare_exchange(expected, next)` must atomically and durably replace
+  the record only if it matches `expected`; NULL means an absent record.
+  It returns `-ESTALE` on mismatch and must preserve the prior committed
+  record on failure. Real hardware must implement that contract including
+  power loss. A backend with ambiguous commit outcomes needs its own
+  transaction resolution before returning to this interface.
+
+Provider bootstrap, master storage, and slot storage must be independent
+of the encrypted `/data` chain. Selecting a wrapped provider makes a
+missing, truncated, invalid, or unauthenticatable slot fail closed. Normal
+start never creates a slot, formats a disk, or falls back to the legacy
+board derivation. Passing NULL selects legacy mode explicitly.
+
+Data keys are retained only while manager-owned volume resources remain
+active and are erased immediately when the last resource is released.
+Temporary master, wrapping key, and rejected plaintext buffers are erased.
+Status exposes only the active wrapping flag, provider key identifier, and
+generation; it exposes no secret key bytes.
+
+## Record format
+
+All integers are little-endian and the layout is serialized explicitly.
+
+| Bytes | Meaning |
+| --- | --- |
+| 0–7 | ASCII `ENCKEY01` |
+| 8–11 | Version 1 |
+| 12–15 | Provider master identifier |
+| 16–23 | Nonzero generation, incremented on rotation |
+| 24–31 | Reserved, must be zero |
+| 32–47 | RFC 5297 AES-SIV authentication tag |
+| 48–111 | Sealed 64-byte stable data keys |
+
+The wrapping key is `HKDF-SHA256(master, salt="encfs-keyslot-v1",
+info=header[0:32], length=32)`. AES-SIV-CMAC-256 uses the full 32-byte header
+as its associated-data string. Existing RFC-vector-tested NuttX crypto
+primitives implement both operations. Generation overflow is rejected.
+
+Generation is authenticated but does not itself prevent rollback. Retaining
+an old valid slot together with its old master still permits recovery of
+the unchanged data keys. Defending against malicious rollback requires
+trusted monotonic state and protection/removal of historical keyslots.
+This implementation promises that the old master cannot authenticate the
+current slot; it does not promise revocation of copies already possessed
+by an attacker or replacement of data keys compromised before rotation.
+
+## Simulator file backend
+
+Only `CONFIG_ENCFS_TESTING` exposes `encfs keyfiles <directory>`. The
+directory must be a dedicated private directory on an independent hostfs
+or 9p mount, such as `/share/keys`, and already exist. This backend is a
+simulator model, **not a TEE or hardware-protected keystore**. The host must
+not concurrently edit the slot or its reserved temporary name. Host master
+files are raw secret bytes, so do not use real production secrets here.
+
+The host fixture generator creates:
+
+* `master-00000000.bin`: 32 random bytes for the initial master;
+* `master-00000001.bin`: 32 different random bytes for its replacement;
+* `volume-random.bin`: 64 fresh random bytes for one provision only.
+
+Use a host cryptographic random source such as Python `os.urandom`, with a
+private directory and restrictive file modes. The backend reads exact
+lengths, rejects malformed files, and consumes `volume-random.bin` once.
+No guest PRNG fallback is used. A repeated destructive provision therefore
+requires a new random fixture. The committed wrapper is `encfs.keyslot`.
+
+On a new, disposable data image:
+
+```text
+encfs keyfiles /share/keys
+encfs provision --erase
+encfs start
+encfs_test rekey 1
+encfs status
+```
+
+The guest rekey test holds an open file, a duplicated descriptor, and a
+directory handle while checking failed and successful rotation, then
+performs a stop/start readback. On later cold boots select the same
+directory and use `encfs start`; do not provision the existing image.
+`encfs testkey board|none|<hex>` explicitly leaves file-provider mode and
+returns to the legacy test/board source, and still requires fully STOPPED.
+
+The backend writes and fsyncs `encfs.keyslot.tmp`, closes it, reads it back,
+and uses the host/9p filesystem's native rename to replace the slot. It
+intentionally bypasses the generic VFS rename path that first removes an
+existing destination. It does not change global hostfs behavior. This
+models atomic replacement under emulator process interruption; it does
+not establish physical host power-loss durability or trusted anti-rollback.
+
+The `.tmp` name is reserved exclusively for this provider. If an emulator
+process is killed before rename, normal start reads the old committed slot.
+A later rotation first compares the committed slot with its expected
+bytes, then automatically removes a recognizable bounded regular staging
+file and retries. A full 112-byte staging record must authenticate and
+contain the current volume's stable data keys. A short 0–111-byte write
+prefix is not authenticated: only a matching magic/version prefix is
+accepted under the dedicated-directory and single-writer contract. A
+bad prefix, oversized file, wrong-volume authenticated slot, or failed
+authentication is retained and reported instead of silently erased. The
+committed slot is never deleted during this recovery.
+
+Test hooks:
+
+```text
+encfs keyslot-failcommit on
+encfs rekey 1
+encfs keyslot-pausecommit 10000
+encfs rekey 1
+```
+
+`failcommit` returns `-EIO` once after staging/readback, preserves the old
+slot, and removes its own temporary file. `pausecommit` pauses once after
+staging/readback and before rename for up to 60,000 milliseconds. A host
+test waits for the serial marker `ENCFS_KEYSLOT_PAUSED
+before_atomic_commit ms=10000` (printed and flushed only after fsync and
+readback), checks the `.tmp` file, kills the emulator process, then verifies
+old-slot cold start and automatic staging recovery. Neither hook simulates
+an actual physical power failure.
+
+## Verification
+
+`tests/test_keywrap.py` compiles the actual manager key/crypto sources under
+ASan and UBSan, exercises normal/failed rotation and legacy compatibility,
+and compares two serialized slots against independent OpenSSL AES-SIV plus
+Python HMAC/HKDF. LeakSanitizer is disabled because the sandbox uses ptrace;
+address and undefined-behavior instrumentation remain enabled.
+
+`tests/test_keywrap_adversarial.py` independently checks every bit of the
+112-byte record, missing/corrupt providers, CAS mismatch, generation
+overflow, staging validation, and data-key output/cache erasure.
+
+`apps/system/encfs/tests/test_rekey_cli.py` compiles the actual CLI with
+production and test configurations and checks strict identifier parsing
+and exclusion of test-provider controls from production builds.
